var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"dofevolvers/#DOF-evolvers","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"A DOF (degree of freedom) evolver turns the force f and torque T accumulated during a timestep into motion, for example v = f/zeta and x += v * dt, and then resets f and T to zero for the next timestep.\n\nPut the DOF evolvers of a simulation in a tuple and pass it to the System:\n\ndofevolvers = (DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf(1), DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc(1))\n\nLike forces, every DOF evolver has an ontypes field with the particle type or types it acts on. Particles that no DOF evolver acts on stay where they are, which is useful for fixed walls.\n\nPosition and orientation are updated by separate evolvers, so a simulation usually has one of each:\n\nSimulation Position Orientation\nOverdamped particles in the xy-plane DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc\nOverdamped particles in 3D DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT\n\nTo write your own, see A custom DOF evolver.","title":"DOF evolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"dofevolvers/#Local-DOF-evolvers","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"Local DOF evolvers update one particle at a time, using only that particle's own fields.\n\nDOF evolver Description\nDOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf Overdamped position update: v = f/zeta, x += v dt, xuw += v dt, then resets f.\nDOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT Overdamped polarity update in 3D: rotates p with angular velocity q = T/zeta_R, then resets T.\nDOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc Overdamped polarity update in the xy-plane: exact rotation about z by T[3]/zeta_R * dt, then resets T.","title":"Local DOF evolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"dofevolvers/#Global-and-field-DOF-evolvers","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"No global DOF evolvers (which need all particles at once) or field DOF evolvers are included yet.","title":"Global and field DOF evolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"dofevolvers/#Abstract-types","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"Type Description\nDOFevolvers.DOFevolver Supertype of all DOF evolvers.\nDOFevolvers.LocalDOFevolver Supertype of local DOF evolvers; describes the method a new one needs.\nDOFevolvers.GlobalDOFevolver Supertype of global DOF evolvers.\nDOFevolvers.FieldDOFevolver Supertype of field DOF evolvers.","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"section","location":"dofevolvers/#Reference","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"overdamped_xvf(ontypes)\n\nOverdamped (Euler–Maruyama) update of the position: v = f / zeta, then x += v * dt and xuw += v * dt, and finally f is reset to zero.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this evolver acts on\n\nRequires particle fields type, zeta, x, xuw, v and f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"overdamped_pqT(ontypes)\n\nOverdamped update of the polarity in 3D: the angular velocity is q = T / zeta_R, the polarity is rotated as p += (q × p) * dt and normalized again, and T is reset to zero.\n\nFor particles that move in the xy-plane, use overdamped_pqT_xyc instead.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this evolver acts on\n\nRequires particle fields type, zeta_R, p, q and T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"overdamped_pqT_xyc(ontypes)\n\nOverdamped update of the polarity for particles in the xy-plane. The polarity is rotated exactly about the z-axis by the angle dθ = T[3] / zeta_R * dt, which keeps p in the xy-plane with unit length. Then q = T / zeta_R and T is reset to zero.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this evolver acts on\n\nRequires particle fields type, zeta_R, p, q and T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.DOFevolver","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"DOFevolver\n\nAbstract supertype of all degree-of-freedom (DOF) evolvers. A DOF evolver turns the forces and torques accumulated during a timestep into new positions, velocities and orientations, and then resets the forces and torques to zero. Put DOF evolver instances in the dofevolvers tuple of a System.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.DOFevolver"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.LocalDOFevolver","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"LocalDOFevolver <: DOFevolver\n\nSupertype of DOF evolvers that update one particle at a time, using only that particle's own fields. A new local DOF evolver needs a method\n\nevolve_locally!(p_i, t, dt, dofevolver::MyEvolver)\n\nthat updates the fields of p_i, resets the forces and torques it used, and returns p_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.LocalDOFevolver"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.GlobalDOFevolver","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"GlobalDOFevolver <: DOFevolver\n\nSupertype of DOF evolvers that need the state of all particles at once. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.GlobalDOFevolver"},{"category":"type","location":"dofevolvers/#JAMS.DOFevolvers.FieldDOFevolver","page":"DOF evolvers","text":"FieldDOFevolver <: DOFevolver\n\nSupertype of DOF evolvers for fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.DOFevolvers.FieldDOFevolver"},{"category":"section","location":"initial_conditions/#Initial-conditions","page":"Initial conditions","text":"The Initial module has helper functions that generate positions for common initial conditions and confining geometries. Each function returns two vectors, xs and ys, with the x- and y-coordinates in the xy-plane, centred on the origin. Use them to build the particles of the initial state:\n\nxs, ys = Initial.random_in_disk(500, 20.0)\n\ninitial_state = ParticleState([\n    Particles.Polar(id=i, type=1, x=[xs[i], ys[i], 0.0], p=normalize([randn(), randn(), 0.0]))\n    for i in eachindex(xs)\n])","title":"Initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"initial_conditions/#Walls-of-fixed-particles","page":"Initial conditions","text":"JAMS has no built-in walls. Instead, you can build a wall from particles that no DOF evolver acts on, so they never move. Initial.box and Initial.ring generate the positions for such walls. For example, active particles (type 1) inside a square box of wall particles (type 2):\n\nLx, Ly = 50.0, 50.0\nxs, ys = Initial.box(1.5, Lx, Ly)            # wall particles about 1.5 apart\nNb = length(xs)\nNint = 1000\n\ninner = [Particles.Polar(id=i, type=1,\n            x=[rand(Uniform(-Lx/2 + 4, Lx/2 - 4)), rand(Uniform(-Ly/2 + 4, Ly/2 - 4)), 0.0],\n            p=normalize([randn(), randn(), 0.0])) for i in 1:Nint]\nwall  = [Particles.Polar(id=Nint + i, type=2, x=[xs[i], ys[i], 0.0], p=[1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n            for i in 1:Nb]\ninitial_state = ParticleState(vcat(inner, wall))\n\nforces = (Forces.self_propulsion(1, 0.01), Forces.repulsive_soft_disk([1, 2], [1.0 1.0; 1.0 1.0]))\ndofevolvers = (DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf(1), DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc(1))  # type 2 stays fixed\n\nMake the simulation box slightly larger than the wall (e.g. sizes = (Lx + 4, Ly + 4, 1.0)) so the wall particles are inside it. Keep the wall particles closer together than the diameter of the inner particles, so none can slip through. See examples/confined_SABPs.jl (square box), examples/confined_SABPs_disk.jl (disk) and examples/confined_SABPs_annulus.jl (annulus) for complete scripts.","title":"Walls of fixed particles"},{"category":"section","location":"initial_conditions/#Available-generators","page":"Initial conditions","text":"Function Generates\nInitial.box positions along the edge of a rectangle, spaced about l apart\nInitial.ring positions on a circle, spaced an arc length of about l apart\nInitial.random_in_disk N random positions spread uniformly over a disk or annulus","title":"Available generators"},{"category":"section","location":"initial_conditions/#Reference","page":"Initial conditions","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"initial_conditions/#JAMS.Initial.box","page":"Initial conditions","text":"box(l, Lx, Ly) -> (xs, ys)\n\nPositions spaced roughly l apart along the edge of a rectangle of size Lx × Ly centred on the origin.\n\nReturns two vectors with the x- and y-coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Initial.box"},{"category":"function","location":"initial_conditions/#JAMS.Initial.ring","page":"Initial conditions","text":"ring(l, R) -> (xs, ys)\n\nPositions on a circle of radius R centred on the origin, spaced an arc length of about l apart. Returns empty vectors if R == 0.\n\nReturns two vectors with the x- and y-coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Initial.ring"},{"category":"function","location":"initial_conditions/#JAMS.Initial.random_in_disk","page":"Initial conditions","text":"random_in_disk(N, R_out; R_in=0) -> (xs, ys)\n\nN random positions distributed uniformly (by area) over a disk of radius R_out centred on the origin, or over the annulus R_in ≤ r ≤ R_out if R_in > 0.\n\nReturns two vectors with the x- and y-coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Initial.random_in_disk"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Particles, forces, DOF evolvers, live plotting functions, save functions and initial condition generators are documented on their own pages: Particles, Forces, DOF evolvers, Live plotting, Save functions and Initial conditions. This page documents the rest, and the index at the bottom lists everything.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Simulation","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API reference","text":"Pages = [\"particles.md\", \"forces.md\", \"dofevolvers.md\", \"live_plotting.md\", \"save_functions.md\", \"initial_conditions.md\", \"api.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JAMS.System","page":"API reference","text":"System(; sizes, initial_particle_state, forces, dofevolvers, Periodic, rcut_pair_global, kwargs...)\n\nEverything that defines a simulation except the integration settings: the box, the initial state, the forces and the DOF evolvers. Pass it to Euler_integrator.\n\nKeywords\n\nsizes: box lengths (Lx, Ly, Lz). The box spans -L/2 to L/2 in each direction.   Always give three lengths; for a 2D simulation, put all particles at z = 0 and use a   small Lz.\ninitial_particle_state: the particles at t = 0, as a ParticleState\nforces: tuple of forces, e.g. (Forces.self_propulsion(1, 0.2), Forces.repulsive_soft_disk(1, 1.0)).   Use a tuple, not a vector: the engine specializes on the exact combination of forces.\ndofevolvers: tuple of DOF evolvers, e.g. (DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf(1),)\nPeriodic: true for periodic boundary conditions in all directions. With false,   particles must stay inside the box; the simulation stops with an error if one leaves it.\nrcut_pair_global: cutoff distance for all pair forces. Pairs further apart are never   evaluated. It also sets the size of the cells used to find neighbours, so choose it as   small as your pair forces allow, e.g. twice the largest radius for soft disks.\ninitial_field_state: tuple of fields (Default: ())\nfield_updaters: tuple of field updaters (Default: ())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.System"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#JAMS.Euler_integrator","page":"API reference","text":"Euler_integrator(system, dt, t_stop; kwargs...) -> SIM\n\nEvolves the initial state defined in system according to the forces and DOFevolvers in it, using the Euler(-Maruyama) algorithm. Returns SIM struct with final states for chaining simulations.\n\nArguments\n\nsystem: Instance of System struct\ndt: Integration time step size\nt_stop: Integration end time\n\nKeywords\n\nseed: master seed for reproducibility   (Default: nothing)\nTsave: Save every Tsave dt-steps   (Default: nothing)\nsave_functions: Tuple of functions to save data   (Default: nothing)\nsave_folder_path: Folder path to save data (need not exist)   (Default: nothing)\nsave_tag: Add custom name to save file name, so that it becomes \"savetag.rawdata.h5\"   (Default: nothing)\nTplot: Plot every Tplot dt-steps   (Default: nothing)\nfps: Default frames per second for rendering of live plotting   (Default: 30)\nplot_functions: Tuple of plot function to live plot   (Default: nothing)\nplotdim: Default plotting dimension, change to 3 for 3d plot   (Default: 2)\nrecord_folder_path: Folder path to save live recording in, need not exist   (Default: nothing)\ncrf: Default compression rate for saving the live recording   (Default: 23)\nres: Pixel resoluation of plotting, e.g. (1000,1000)   (Default: nothing)\nformat: Default format for saving live recording   (Default: \"mp4\")\nsbs: Set to true for side-by-side plotting, to be used i.c.m. with plotdim=3   (Default: false)\n\nOutput files\n\nWith Tsave, save_functions and save_folder_path set, three files are written to save_folder_path (prefixed with save_tag_ if save_tag is given):\n\nraw_data.h5: HDF5 file with the parameters of the system, forces and DOF evolvers, and   one group frames/1, frames/2, ... per saved frame, filled by the save_functions\nJAMs_container.jld2: the System and integration settings (Julia objects)\nJAMs_final_state.jld2: the final state as a JAMS.SIM under the key \"SIM\"\n\nThe simulation refuses to start if these files already exist, so nothing is overwritten.\n\nLive plotting (Tplot, plot_functions) requires using GLMakie after using JAMS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Euler_integrator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JAMS.SIM","page":"API reference","text":"SIM\n\nResult of Euler_integrator. Use it to inspect the final state, or to start a new simulation from where the previous one ended (see Chaining simulations in the docs).\n\nFields\n\nfinal_particle_state: particle state at the end of the simulation\nfinal_field_state: field state at the end of the simulation\ndt: timestep used\nt_stop: end time used\nsystem: the System that was simulated (it still contains the initial state)\nfinished: true if the simulation ran to the end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.SIM"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#JAMS.ParticleState","page":"API reference","text":"ParticleState(particles)\n\nAlias for StructArray from StructArrays.jl. Use it to turn a vector of particles into the state that a System needs:\n\ninitial_state = ParticleState([Particles.Polar(id=i, type=1, x=..., p=...) for i in 1:N])\n\nEach particle field becomes a column, so initial_state.x is the vector of all positions, and initial_state[i] returns particle i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.ParticleState"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"In a Julia script or in the Julia REPL, run\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/sanderkammeraat/JAMS.jl\")\n\nand load the package with\n\nusing JAMS\n\nusing JAMS also loads Random, Distributions and LinearAlgebra, so functions such as rand(Uniform(0, 1)) and normalize are available straight away.\n\nJAMS runs in parallel on as many threads as Julia is started with. If one uses VS Code, the number of threads used by Julia are set automatically.  Running from the terminal, you have to specify the number of threads manually. To start Julia with, for example, 4 threads, do:\n\njulia -t 4","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#An-example-simulation:-Active-Brownian-particles","page":"Getting started","text":"This example simulates 1000 active Brownian particles (ABPs) in a periodic 2D box. Each particle moves with a constant speed along its polarity vector, the polarity diffuses randomly, and overlapping particles repel each other according to a soft disk force.","title":"An example simulation: Active Brownian particles"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#1.-Choose-the-forces","page":"Getting started","text":"using JAMS \nforces = (\n    Forces.self_propulsion(1, 0.2),                      # speed v0 = 0.2\n    Forces.planar_rotational_noise(ontypes=1, Dr=0.01),  # rotational diffusion\n    Forces.repulsive_soft_disk(1, 1.0),                  # harmonic repulsion, stiffness 1\n)\n\nThe first argument of every force, ontypes, lists the particle types it acts on. Here all particles have type 1. As you can see, the definition for forces in JAMS is a bit loose: for example the rotational  noise is strictly a torque instead of a force.","title":"1. Choose the forces"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#2.-Choose-the-DOF-evolvers","page":"Getting started","text":"A DOF (degree of freedom) evolver takes the total force and torque and applies it to a particle. For overdamped particles in the xy-plane, use one evolver for the position and one for the polarity:\n\ndofevolvers = (\n    DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf(1),       # x += f/zeta * dt\n    DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc(1),   # rotate p by T/zeta_R * dt about z\n)","title":"2. Choose the DOF evolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#3.-Setup-the-initial-particle-condition","page":"Getting started","text":"N = 1000\nϕ = 1.0                                   # packing fraction\npoly = 0.15                               # polydispersity of the radii\nRs = rand(Uniform(1 - poly, 1 + poly), N)\nL = sqrt(pi * sum(Rs .^ 2) / ϕ)           # box size for packing fraction ϕ\n\ninitial_state = ParticleState([\n    Particles.Polar(\n        id = i,\n        type = 1,\n        R = Rs[i],\n        x = [rand(Uniform(-L/2, L/2)), rand(Uniform(-L/2, L/2)), 0.0],\n        p = normalize([randn(), randn(), 0.0]),\n    )\n    for i in 1:N\n])\n\nPositions always have three components. For a 2D simulation, set the z-component to 0. Particle ids must run from 1 to N.","title":"3. Setup the initial particle condition"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#4.-Define-the-system","page":"Getting started","text":"system = System(\n    sizes = (L, L, 2.0),\n    initial_particle_state = initial_state,\n    forces = forces,\n    dofevolvers = dofevolvers,\n    Periodic = true,\n    rcut_pair_global = 2 * (1 + poly),     # largest possible R_i + R_j\n)\n\nThe box runs from -L/2 to L/2 in each direction. rcut_pair_global is the largest distance at which pair forces are evaluated. Soft disks only touch below R_i + R_j, so twice the largest radius is enough; a larger cutoff gives the same result but is slower.","title":"4. Define the system"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#5.-Run-it","page":"Getting started","text":"sim = Euler_integrator(system, 0.01, 100.0; seed=42)\n\nThis integrates from t = 0 to t = 100 with timestep dt = 0.01, showing a progress bar. The result is a JAMS.SIM:\n\nsim.final_particle_state.x        # final positions of all particles\nsim.final_particle_state[1]       # final state of particle 1\n\nWith the same seed, the simulation gives the same result every time, also with a different number of threads.","title":"5. Run it"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Saving-data","page":"Getting started","text":"To write data to disk, give a save interval, one or more save functions and a folder:\n\nsim = Euler_integrator(system, 0.01, 100.0;\n    seed = 42,\n    Tsave = 100,                              # save every 100 timesteps\n    save_functions = (Save.polar_particle!,),\n    save_folder_path = joinpath(pwd(), \"ABP_example\"),\n)\n\nThis creates ABP_example/raw_data.h5 with one group per saved frame (frames/1, frames/2, ...). Read it back with HDF5.jl or more conveniently in Julia using JLD2:\n\nJAMS prevents writing into a folder that already contains output files, so an earlier run is never overwritten. Use a new folder, or set save_tag to give the files a prefix. See Euler_integrator for all output files.","title":"Saving data"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Live-plotting","page":"Getting started","text":"For live plotting, load GLMakie after JAMS:\n\nusing JAMS\nusing GLMakie\n\nThis enables the optional live plotting extension of JAMS and compiles it. and pass a plot interval and plot functions:\n\nsim = Euler_integrator(system, 0.01, 1000.0;\n    Tplot = 10,                                                #update plot every 10 time steps\n    plot_functions = (LPlot.disks_orientation!, LPlot.directors!),\n)\n\nThis opens a window that shows the particles as disks coloured by their orientation, with arrows along their polarity. Closing the window stops the simulation. To record a video, also set record_folder_path. The LPlot module lists all plot functions.\n\nOn a cluster without a display, leave out using GLMakie and Tplot.","title":"Live plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"Overview explains the building blocks and the order of operations in a timestep.\nExtending JAMS shows how to write your own forces and update rules.\nThe examples/ folder has complete scripts, including the one above with self-alignment (examples/ABPs.jl).","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"save_functions/#Save-functions","page":"Save functions","text":"Save functions write the particle (and field) state to disk during a simulation. Pass a tuple of them to Euler_integrator, together with a save interval and a folder:\n\nsim = Euler_integrator(system, 0.01, 100.0;\n    Tsave = 100,                                  # save every 100 timesteps\n    save_functions = (Save.polar_particle!,),\n    save_folder_path = joinpath(pwd(), \"run1\"),\n    save_tag = \"abp\",                             # optional prefix for the file names\n)\n\nEvery Tsave timesteps, JAMS creates a new group frames/1, frames/2, ... in the HDF5 file raw_data.h5 and calls each save function with that group. The save function decides which datasets to write. The saved positions are those at the start of the timestep, before they are updated.\n\nBesides raw_data.h5, JAMS writes JAMs_container.jld2 (the System and integration settings) and JAMs_final_state.jld2 (the final state). With save_tag, all three file names get the tag as a prefix, e.g. abp_raw_data.h5. JAMS refuses to start if these files already exist in the folder, so earlier results are never overwritten.","title":"Save functions"},{"category":"section","location":"save_functions/#Reading-the-data-back","page":"Save functions","text":"using HDF5\n\nh5open(joinpath(\"run1\", \"abp_raw_data.h5\"), \"r\") do file\n    nframes = length(file[\"frames\"])\n    for k in 1:nframes\n        frame = file[\"frames/$k\"]\n        t = read(frame[\"t\"])\n        x = read(frame[\"x\"])\n        y = read(frame[\"y\"])\n        # ... analyse frame k\n    end\nend\n\nThe file also contains the parameters of the system, forces and DOF evolvers, and the integration settings (under system and integration_info), so each output file describes the simulation that produced it.","title":"Reading the data back"},{"category":"section","location":"save_functions/#Available-save-functions","page":"Save functions","text":"Save function Saves For particles with fields\nSave.polar_particle! n, t, id, type, R, position, unwrapped position, velocity, polarity and angular velocity, split into x-, y- and z-components id, type, R, x, xuw, v, p, q\n\nTo save other quantities, or for your own particle type, see A custom save function.","title":"Available save functions"},{"category":"section","location":"save_functions/#Reference","page":"Save functions","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"save_functions/#JAMS.Save.polar_particle!","page":"Save functions","text":"polar_particle!(current_frame_group, current_particle_state, current_field_state, n, Tsave, t, framecounter)\n\nSave function for Particles.Polar-like particles. Pass it to Euler_integrator as save_functions=(Save.polar_particle!,). Every saved frame, it writes these datasets to the frame's HDF5 group:\n\nn, t: step number and time\nid, type, R\nx, y, z: position\nxuw, yuw, zuw: unwrapped position\nvx, vy, vz: velocity\npx, py, pz: polarity\nqx, qy, qz: angular velocity\n\nEach dataset has one entry per particle, in the order of the particle state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Save.polar_particle!"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Live-plotting","page":"Live plotting","text":"JAMS can show the simulation while it runs, using GLMakie. This is useful for quickly seeing how a model behaves for different parameters. Load GLMakie after JAMS to enable it:\n\nusing JAMS\nusing GLMakie\n\nThen give Euler_integrator a plot interval and a tuple of plot functions:\n\nsim = Euler_integrator(system, 0.01, 1000.0;\n    Tplot = 10,                                                  # redraw every 10 timesteps\n    fps = 60,\n    plot_functions = (LPlot.disks_orientation!, LPlot.directors!),\n)\n\nThe plot functions are drawn on top of each other in the order given, so here the particles are drawn as disks coloured by orientation, with arrows along their polarity on top. Closing the window stops the simulation.\n\nOther plotting keywords of Euler_integrator:\n\nplotdim = 3: use a 3D axis, for 3D simulations (Default: 2)\nsbs = true: with plotdim = 3, show two side-by-side 3D views for stereo viewing\nres = (1000, 1000): window size in pixels\nrecord_folder_path: also record the plot to movie.mp4 in this folder; set the format with format and the compression with crf\n\nOn a cluster without a display, leave out using GLMakie and Tplot.\n\nEach plot function only works for particles (or fields) that have the fields it uses. The tables below list them, and the LPlot module docstring describes the signature for writing your own; see also A custom plot function.","title":"Live plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Particles-as-points","page":"Live plotting","text":"Plot function Shows Needs particle fields\nLPlot.points! positions, coloured by id x, id\nLPlot.type_points! positions, coloured by type x, type\nLPlot.director_points! markers at the tip of the polarity, x + p x, p, id\nLPlot.trajectories! the last 100 positions of every particle as lines (experimental) x\nLPlot.Swarmalators! positions, coloured by internal phase x, ϕ","title":"Particles as points"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Particles-at-their-true-size","page":"Live plotting","text":"Plot function Shows Needs particle fields\nLPlot.disks! disks of radius R, coloured by id x, R, id\nLPlot.disks_type! disks, coloured by type x, R, type\nLPlot.disks_orientation! disks, coloured by polarity angle x, R, p\nLPlot.disks_nematic_orientation! disks, coloured by nematic angle (p and -p same colour) x, R, p\nLPlot.disks_v_orientation! disks, coloured by velocity direction x, R, v\nLPlot.disks_vp_phase_difference! disks, coloured by the angle between v and p x, R, v, p\nLPlot.disks_vx! disks, coloured by the x-component of the velocity x, R, v\nLPlot.disks_uw! disks at the unwrapped positions xuw, R, id\nLPlot.transparant_disks! white, almost transparent disks with an outline x, R\nLPlot.sized_points! disks in 2D, transparent spheres in 3D, coloured by id x, R, id\nLPlot.type_sized_points! disks in 2D, spheres in 3D, coloured by type x, R, type\nLPlot.polymers! disks, coloured by polymer x, R, pol_id\nLPlot.polymers_3d! spheres, coloured by polymer (plotdim = 3) x, R, pol_id\nLPlot.ellipses! ellipses from a shape tensor, rotated along the polarity x, p, Lambda\nLPlot.sphere! a white background sphere, for particles confined to a sphere (plotdim = 3) x","title":"Particles at their true size"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Rigid-bodies","page":"Live plotting","text":"Plot function Shows Needs particle fields\nLPlot.shape_disks! circles at the extent points, coloured by id xe, re, id\nLPlot.shape_disks_orientation! circles at the extent points, coloured by polarity angle xe, re, p\nLPlot.shape_disks_type! circles at the extent points, coloured by type xe, re, type\nLPlot.shape_points! points at the extent points, coloured by id xe, re, id","title":"Rigid bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Vectors-and-labels","page":"Live plotting","text":"Plot function Shows Needs particle fields\nLPlot.directors! arrows along the polarity x, p\nLPlot.nematic_directors! double-headed arrows along p and -p x, p\nLPlot.velocity_vectors! arrows along the velocity x, v\nLPlot.annotate_v! the speed of every particle as a text label x, v","title":"Vectors and labels"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Fields","page":"Live plotting","text":"These plot the first field of the field state.\n\nPlot function Shows Needs field fields\nLPlot.field_magnitude! heatmap of C (range 0–2) with the bin edges bin_centers, lbin, C\nLPlot.GPUfield_magnitude! as field_magnitude!, for single-precision or GPU fields bin_centers, lbin, C\nLPlot.field_log_magnitude! heatmap of log10(C) (range -4 to -2) bin_centers, C\nLPlot.field_magnitude_wgrid! heatmap of C (range 0–1) with grid lines at the bin centres bin_centers, C\nLPlot.potential! surface plot of C as an energy landscape (plotdim = 3) bin_centers, C","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Reference","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Particles-as-points-2","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Particles as points"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Particles-at-their-true-size-2","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Particles at their true size"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Rigid-bodies-2","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Rigid bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Vectors-and-labels-2","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Vectors and labels"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Fields-2","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"live_plotting/#Helpers","page":"Live plotting","text":"","title":"Helpers"},{"category":"module","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot","page":"Live plotting","text":"LPlot\n\nPlot functions for live plotting. Pass a tuple of them to Euler_integrator as plot_functions, e.g. plot_functions=(LPlot.disks_orientation!, LPlot.directors!). Live plotting needs using GLMakie after using JAMS.\n\nEvery plot function has the signature f(fig, ax, cpsO, cfsO), where cpsO and cfsO are Makie Observables of the current particle state and field state. Each function only works for particles (or fields) that have the fields it uses, listed in its docstring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nPositions, coloured by particle id. Requires particle fields x and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.type_points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"type_points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nPositions, coloured by particle type. Requires particle fields x and type.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.type_points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.director_points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"director_points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nMarkers at the tip of the polarity vector, x + p, in red (for 3-component positions). Requires particle fields x, p and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.director_points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.trajectories!","page":"Live plotting","text":"trajectories!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nExperimental. The last 100 positions of every particle, drawn as lines. Requires particle field x. Assumes the particle order does not change during the simulation.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.trajectories!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.Swarmalators!","page":"Live plotting","text":"Swarmalators!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nPositions, coloured by the internal phase ϕ (swarmalator models). Requires particle fields x and ϕ.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.Swarmalators!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R at the particle positions, coloured by id. Requires particle fields x, R and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_type!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_type!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by particle type. Requires particle fields x, R and type.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_type!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_orientation!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_orientation!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by the angle of the polarity p in the xy-plane. Requires particle fields x, R and p.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_orientation!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_nematic_orientation!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_nematic_orientation!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by the nematic angle of the polarity (the angle of p doubled, so p and -p get the same colour). Requires particle fields x, R and p.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_nematic_orientation!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_v_orientation!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_v_orientation!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by the direction of the velocity v in the xy-plane. Requires particle fields x, R and v.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_v_orientation!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_vp_phase_difference!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_vp_phase_difference!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by the angle between the velocity v and the polarity p. Requires particle fields x, R, v and p.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_vp_phase_difference!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_vx!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_vx!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by the x-component of the velocity (colour range ±0.01). Requires particle fields x, R and v.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_vx!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.disks_uw!","page":"Live plotting","text":"disks_uw!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R at the unwrapped positions xuw, coloured by id. Requires particle fields xuw, R and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.disks_uw!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.transparant_disks!","page":"Live plotting","text":"transparant_disks!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nWhite, almost transparent disks of radius R with a thin outline. Requires particle fields x and R.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.transparant_disks!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.sized_points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"sized_points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nParticles drawn at their true size: disks of radius R in 2D, transparent spheres in 3D, coloured by id. Requires particle fields x, R and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.sized_points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.type_sized_points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"type_sized_points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nParticles drawn at their true size (disks in 2D, spheres in 3D), coloured by type. Requires particle fields x, R and type.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.type_sized_points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.polymers!","page":"Live plotting","text":"polymers!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDisks of radius R, coloured by polymer id pol_id. Requires particle fields x, R and pol_id, as in Particles.PolarPolymer.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.polymers!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.polymers_3d!","page":"Live plotting","text":"polymers_3d!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nSpheres of radius R, coloured by polymer id pol_id. Use with plotdim=3. Requires particle fields x, R and pol_id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.polymers_3d!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.ellipses!","page":"Live plotting","text":"ellipses!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nParticles drawn as ellipses whose axes are the eigenvalues of the 2×2 shape tensor Lambda, rotated along the polarity p. Requires particle fields x, p and Lambda.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.ellipses!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.sphere!","page":"Live plotting","text":"sphere!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nA white sphere centred on the origin, with radius equal to the distance of the first particle from the origin. Useful as a background for particles confined to a sphere. Use with plotdim=3.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.sphere!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks!","page":"Live plotting","text":"shape_disks!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nRigid bodies drawn as circles at their extent points xe with radii re, coloured by id. Requires particle fields xe, re and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks_orientation!","page":"Live plotting","text":"shape_disks_orientation!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nLike shape_disks!, coloured by the angle of the polarity p in the xy-plane.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks_orientation!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks_type!","page":"Live plotting","text":"shape_disks_type!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nLike shape_disks!, coloured by particle type.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.shape_disks_type!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.shape_points!","page":"Live plotting","text":"shape_points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nRigid bodies drawn as points at their extent points xe, coloured by id. Requires particle fields xe, re and id.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.shape_points!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.directors!","page":"Live plotting","text":"directors!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nArrows along the polarity p at every particle, coloured by the angle of p in the xy-plane. Requires particle fields x and p.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.directors!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.nematic_directors!","page":"Live plotting","text":"nematic_directors!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nDouble-headed arrows along p and -p at every particle, coloured by the nematic angle. Requires particle fields x and p.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.nematic_directors!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.velocity_vectors!","page":"Live plotting","text":"velocity_vectors!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nArrows along the velocity v at every particle, coloured by the direction of v. Requires particle fields x and v.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.velocity_vectors!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.annotate_v!","page":"Live plotting","text":"annotate_v!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nThe speed |v| of every particle as a text label next to it. Requires particle fields x and v.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.annotate_v!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.field_magnitude!","page":"Live plotting","text":"field_magnitude!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nHeatmap of the concentration C of the first field, with colour range 0 to 2 and the bin edges drawn as grid lines. Requires a field with fields bin_centers, lbin and C.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.field_magnitude!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.GPUfield_magnitude!","page":"Live plotting","text":"GPUfield_magnitude!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nSame as field_magnitude!, but converts C to Float64 first, for fields stored in single precision or on a GPU.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.GPUfield_magnitude!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.field_log_magnitude!","page":"Live plotting","text":"field_log_magnitude!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nHeatmap of log10(C) of the first field, with colour range -4 to -2. Requires a field with fields bin_centers and C.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.field_log_magnitude!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.field_magnitude_wgrid!","page":"Live plotting","text":"field_magnitude_wgrid!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nHeatmap of C of the first field, with colour range 0 to 1 and grid lines at the bin centres. Requires a field with fields bin_centers and C.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.field_magnitude_wgrid!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.potential!","page":"Live plotting","text":"potential!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n\nSurface plot of C of the first field, interpreted as a potential energy landscape. Use with plotdim=3. Requires a field with fields bin_centers and C.\n\nLive plot function: pass it to Euler_integrator in plot_functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.potential!"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.ellipse","page":"Live plotting","text":"ellipse(p_i) -> (xs, ys)\n\nOutline of the ellipse used by ellipses! for particle p_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.ellipse"},{"category":"function","location":"live_plotting/#JAMS.LPlot.angle2range","page":"Live plotting","text":"angle2range(angle)\n\nMap an angle in radians to the range [0, 2π).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.LPlot.angle2range"},{"category":"section","location":"particles/#Particles","page":"Particles","text":"A particle type is an immutable struct that holds the degrees of freedom and parameters of one particle. All particles of a simulation are stored together in a ParticleState, with one column per field, so state.x is the vector of all positions.\n\nCreate particles with keyword arguments and collect them in a ParticleState:\n\ninitial_state = ParticleState([\n    Particles.Polar(id=i, type=1, x=[0.0, 0.0, 0.0], p=[1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n    for i in 1:N\n])\n\nParticle ids must run from 1 to N. Vectors such as x always have three components, also in 2D simulations.\n\nTo define your own particle type, see A custom particle type.","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"particles/#Available-particles","page":"Particles","text":"Particle Description\nParticles.Polar Spherical particle with a position and a polarity vector. The standard choice for active Brownian particles and soft disks.\nParticles.PolarPolymer Polar particle that is one monomer of a polymer, with the polymer id, its position along the polymer and the polymer length.","title":"Available particles"},{"category":"section","location":"particles/#Abstract-types","page":"Particles","text":"Type Description\nParticles.Particle Supertype of all particle types.\nParticles.RigidBody Supertype of extended (rigid-body) particles.","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"section","location":"particles/#Reference","page":"Particles","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"particles/#JAMS.Particles.Polar","page":"Particles","text":"Polar(; id, type, x, p, kwargs...)\n\nSpherical particle with a position and a polarity (orientation) vector. Construct it with keyword arguments; fields with a default value can be left out.\n\nFields\n\nid::Int64: unique particle number. Ids must run from 1 to N, because each particle's   random number generator is looked up by id.\ntype::Int64: particle type. Forces and DOF evolvers only act on the types listed in   their ontypes field.\nm::Float64: mass (Default: 1.0)\nzeta::Float64: translational friction coefficient (Default: 1.0)\nzeta_R::Float64: rotational friction coefficient (Default: 1.0)\nR::Float64: radius (Default: 1.0)\nx::SVector{3,Float64}: position, kept inside the box by periodic boundary conditions\nxuw::SVector{3,Float64}: unwrapped position, not affected by periodic boundary conditions.   It is set equal to x at the start of a simulation. (Default: zeros)\nv::SVector{3,Float64}: velocity (Default: zeros)\nf::SVector{3,Float64}: total force. The forces add to it and the DOF evolvers reset it   every timestep. (Default: zeros)\np::SVector{3,Float64}: polarity vector (unit vector)\nq::SVector{3,Float64}: angular velocity; for particles moving in the xy-plane only the   z-component is nonzero (Default: zeros)\nT::SVector{3,Float64}: total torque, accumulated and reset like f (Default: zeros)\n\nExample\n\np = Particles.Polar(id=1, type=1, R=1.0, x=[0.0, 0.0, 0.0], p=[1.0, 0.0, 0.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Particles.Polar"},{"category":"type","location":"particles/#JAMS.Particles.PolarPolymer","page":"Particles","text":"PolarPolymer(; id, type, pol_id, id_in_pol, pol_N, x, p, kwargs...)\n\nPolar particle that is one monomer of a polymer. It has all fields of Polar, plus three fields that describe its place in the polymer:\n\npol_id::Int64: id of the polymer this particle belongs to\nid_in_pol::Int64: position of the particle along the polymer\npol_N::Int64: number of particles in the polymer\n\nLPlot.polymers! colours particles by polymer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Particles.PolarPolymer"},{"category":"type","location":"particles/#JAMS.Particles.Particle","page":"Particles","text":"Particle\n\nAbstract supertype of all particle types. A concrete particle type is an immutable struct whose fields are the degrees of freedom and parameters of one particle. The simulation stores all particles in a ParticleState (a StructArray), so every field becomes a column that forces and DOF evolvers can read and update.\n\nA particle type needs at least the fields id, type, x, xuw, f and T; see the Extending JAMS page for details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Particles.Particle"},{"category":"type","location":"particles/#JAMS.Particles.RigidBody","page":"Particles","text":"RigidBody\n\nAbstract supertype for extended (rigid-body) particles. Periodic boundary conditions are only applied to their center position x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Particles.RigidBody"},{"category":"section","location":"#JAMS.jl","page":"Home","text":"Jamming and Active Matter Simulations in Julia\n\nJAMS is a Julia package to simulate (Soft) Active Matter. Its design is modular, so that you can mix and match different forces, particles and fields.\n\nThe purpose of this package is twofold: on one hand it provides a convenient way to explore new active matter models by providing flexible construction of (types of) forces and particles (e.g. simple polar particles or polymers). The exploration is facilated by an optional live plotting extension, leveraging GLMakie's efficient GPU plotting to render e.g. particle's positions, velocity vectors or polarties to quickly gauge what the system behaves like for different parameter values.\n\nThe second is to be performant to run production simulations for actual scientific analysis. The package has been through extensive profiling, is multi-threaded and easily runs on head-less clusters. Output is stored in the HDF5 format.","title":"JAMS.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"Getting started installs JAMS and walks through a first simulation of Active Brownian particles.\nOverview provieds an overview of the JAMS code, specifically how particles, forces, DOF evolvers and the System struct fit together.\nParticles, Forces, DOF evolvers, Live plotting, Save functions and Initial conditions list all available particle types, forces, DOF evolvers, live plotting functions, save functions and initial condition generators.\nExtending JAMS shows how new particles, forces and DOFevolvers etc. can be added.\nAPI reference lists every type and function.\n\nThe examples/ folder of the repository contains complete scripts, such as active Brownian particles, active particles in a soft gel, and confined self-aligning particles.\n\nPages = [\"getting_started.md\", \"overview.md\", \"particles.md\", \"forces.md\", \"dofevolvers.md\", \"live_plotting.md\", \"save_functions.md\", \"initial_conditions.md\", \"extending.md\", \"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Forces","page":"Forces","text":"Forces add to the total force f and torque T of particles every timestep. They never move particles themselves; that is the job of the DOF evolvers.\n\nPut the forces of a simulation in a tuple and pass it to the System:\n\nforces = (\n    Forces.self_propulsion(1, 0.2),\n    Forces.planar_rotational_noise(ontypes=1, Dr=0.01),\n    Forces.repulsive_soft_disk(1, 1.0),\n)\n\nThe first field of every force, ontypes, is the particle type (an Int) or types (a Vector{Int}) it acts on. A pair force only acts between two particles if both have a type in ontypes.\n\nThere are three kinds of forces:\n\nExternal forces act on one particle at a time.\nPair forces act between two particles closer than rcut_pair_global.\nField forces act between particles and fields.\n\nTo write your own, see A custom external force and A custom pair force.","title":"Forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#External-forces","page":"Forces","text":"Force Description\nForces.self_propulsion Constant force zeta * v0 * p along the polarity, giving self-propulsion speed v0.\nForces.planar_rotational_noise Gaussian white-noise torque about a fixed axis, giving rotational diffusion of the polarity.\nForces.self_align_with_v Torque that rotates the polarity towards the particle's own velocity.","title":"External forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Pair-forces","page":"Forces","text":"Force Description\nForces.repulsive_soft_disk Harmonic repulsion between overlapping particles, U = k/2 (R_i + R_j - r)^2.\nForces.morse Morse potential: repulsive below contact, attractive beyond it, up to rcut_pair_global.","title":"Pair forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Field-forces","page":"Forces","text":"No field forces are included yet.","title":"Field forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Abstract-types","page":"Forces","text":"Type Description\nForces.Force Supertype of all forces.\nForces.ExternalForce Supertype of external forces; describes the method a new external force needs.\nForces.PairForce Supertype of pair forces; describes the method a new pair force needs.\nForces.FieldForce Supertype of field forces.","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Reference","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#External-forces-2","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"External forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Pair-forces-2","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"Pair forces"},{"category":"section","location":"forces/#Abstract-types-2","page":"Forces","text":"","title":"Abstract types"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.self_propulsion","page":"Forces","text":"self_propulsion(ontypes, v0)\nself_propulsion(; ontypes, v0)\n\nConstant self-propulsion force along the polarity vector: f += zeta * v0 * p. For an overdamped particle this gives a self-propulsion speed v0.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this force acts on\nv0: self-propulsion speed\n\nRequires particle fields type, zeta, p and f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.self_propulsion"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.planar_rotational_noise","page":"Forces","text":"planar_rotational_noise(; ontypes, Dr, normal=[0, 0, 1])\n\nGaussian white-noise torque about the axis normal: T += sqrt(2Dr) * η  sqrt(dt) * normal, with η a standard normal random number drawn from the particle's own random number generator every timestep.\n\nCombined with DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc, the polarity angle in the xy-plane then diffuses with rotational diffusion coefficient Dr / zeta_R^2 (so Dr for the default zeta_R = 1).\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this force acts on\nDr: noise strength (rotational diffusion coefficient for zeta_R = 1)\nnormal: rotation axis (Default: [0, 0, 1], rotations in the xy-plane)\n\nRequires particle fields id, type and T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.planar_rotational_noise"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.self_align_with_v","page":"Forces","text":"self_align_with_v(ontypes, J, unit)\n\nTorque that rotates the polarity p towards the particle's own velocity v (self-alignment): T += J * (p × v), or T += J * (p × v / |v|) if unit is true. The velocity used is the one set by the DOF evolver in the previous timestep, so this force does nothing in the first timestep if the particles start with v = 0.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this force acts on\nJ: alignment strength\nunit: if true, use the direction of v only (the torque does not grow with speed)\n\nRequires particle fields type, p, v and T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.self_align_with_v"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.repulsive_soft_disk","page":"Forces","text":"repulsive_soft_disk(ontypes, karray)\nrepulsive_soft_disk(; ontypes, karray)\n\nHarmonic repulsion between overlapping particles. Two particles at distance r interact when r < R_i + R_j, with potential energy U = k/2 * (R_i + R_j - r)^2, where k = karray[type_i, type_j]. There is no force between particles that do not overlap.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this force acts on. Both   particles must have a type in ontypes.\nkarray: stiffness, a matrix indexed by the two particle types, e.g. [1.0 2.0; 2.0 2.0]   for types 1 and 2. A plain number only works if all particles have type 1.\n\nRequires particle fields type, R and f. Set rcut_pair_global in the System to at least twice the largest radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.repulsive_soft_disk"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.morse","page":"Forces","text":"morse(ontypes, Dearray, aarray)\nmorse(; ontypes, Dearray, aarray)\n\nMorse potential between particles, attractive beyond contact and repulsive below it: U = De * (1 - exp(-a * (r - r_e)))^2, with equilibrium distance r_e = R_i + R_j, De = Dearray[type_i, type_j] and a = aarray[type_i, type_j].\n\nThe force acts on every pair closer than rcut_pair_global, so that cutoff sets the range of the attraction.\n\nFields\n\nontypes: particle type (Int) or types (Vector{Int}) this force acts on. Both   particles must have a type in ontypes.\nDearray: well depth De, a matrix indexed by the two particle types\naarray: inverse width a, a matrix indexed by the two particle types\n\nA plain number instead of a matrix only works if all particles have type 1.\n\nRequires particle fields type, R and f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.morse"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.Force","page":"Forces","text":"Force\n\nAbstract supertype of all forces. Put force instances in the forces tuple of a System. The engine sorts them by subtype (ExternalForce, PairForce or FieldForce) and calls the matching method every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.Force"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.ExternalForce","page":"Forces","text":"ExternalForce <: Force\n\nSupertype of forces and torques that act on one particle at a time, such as self-propulsion or rotational noise. A new external force needs a method\n\ncontribute_external_force!(p_i, t, dt, rngs_particles, system, force::MyForce)\n\nthat adds to p_i.f and/or p_i.T and returns p_i. Here p_i is a mutable view of one particle, t the current time, dt the timestep, rngs_particles[p_i.id] the random number generator of this particle and system the System.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.ExternalForce"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.PairForce","page":"Forces","text":"PairForce <: Force\n\nSupertype of forces between pairs of particles. A new pair force needs a method\n\ncontribute_pair_force!(p_i, p_j, dx, dxn, t, dt, rngs_particles, system, force::MyForce)\n\nthat adds the force of particle j on particle i to p_i.f (and optionally a torque to p_i.T) and returns p_i. Here dx = x_j - x_i is the separation vector (minimal image if the system is periodic) and dxn its length. The method is only called for pairs with dxn <= system.rcut_pair_global. It is called separately for (i, j) and (j, i), so it must only change p_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.PairForce"},{"category":"type","location":"forces/#JAMS.Forces.FieldForce","page":"Forces","text":"FieldForce <: Force\n\nSupertype of forces between particles and fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JAMS.Forces.FieldForce"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Overview","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"JAMS is modular thanks to its abstraction of the general building blocks that are naturally part of a simulation. This page explains what each abstract building block is and how together they define a complete simulation in JAMS.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Building-blocks","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"Building block What it is Examples\nParticle type An immutable struct with the fields of one particle Particles.Polar\nParticle state All particles, stored column by column ParticleState\nForce Adds to the force f and/or torque T of particles Forces.self_propulsion, Forces.repulsive_soft_disk\nDOF evolver Takes the total force and torque f and T of particle and evolves the particle, then resets them DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf\nSystem Defines the system: sets the system sizes, the initial state, the forces and the DOF evolvers System\nIntegrator Taking in System, it runs the simulation and returns the final state Euler_integrator","title":"Building blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Particles-and-the-particle-state","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"A particle type such as Particles.Polar is an immutable struct: id, type, radius R, position x, polarity p, force f, and so on. Vectors are 3-component SVectors from StaticArrays.jl, also in 2D simulations.\n\nAll particles together form a ParticleState, which is a StructArray. It stores each field as its own column: state.x is the vector of all positions, state.R the vector of all radii. This is used for peformance.","title":"Particles and the particle state"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Particle-types-and-ontypes","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"Every particle has an integer type. Every force and DOF evolver has an ontypes field with the type (an Int) or types (a Vector{Int}) it acts on. This is how you give different particles different physics. For example, in examples/confined_SABPs.jl the active particles have type 1 and the pinned particles type 2:\n\nforces = (\n    Forces.self_propulsion(1, 0.01),                   # only type 1 is active\n    Forces.repulsive_soft_disk([1, 2], [1 2; 2 2]),    # all particles repel each other, k_11 =1, k_12 =2, k_21 =2, k_22=2 \n)\ndofevolvers = (DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf(1),)         # only type 1 moves\n\nParticles that no DOF evolver acts on never move, so the type-2 particles form a fixed boundary.\n\nFor pair forces, both particles must have a type in ontypes. Parameters that depend on the pair of types, such as the stiffness of Forces.repulsive_soft_disk, can be given as a matrix indexed by the two types.","title":"Particle types and ontypes"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Forces","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"There are currently three kinds of forces, each with its own abstract type:\n\nForces.ExternalForce: acts on one particle at a time, e.g. self-propulsion, noise or an external field. It can be calculated for each particle independently.\nForces.PairForce: acts between two particles closer than rcut_pair_global, e.g. soft repulsion. It needs the relative positions of two particles.\nForces.FieldForce: acts between particles and fields.\n\nForces only add to f and T. They never move particles themselves.","title":"Forces"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#DOF-evolvers","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"A DOF (Degree Of Freedom) evolver updates a particle's state from the accumulated f and T, for example v = f/ζ and x += v dt for an overdamped particle, and then sets f and T back to zero for the next timestep. Because the forces and the equations of motion are separate, you can reuse the same forces with overdamped or inertial dynamics.\n\nA position evolver and an orientation evolver are independent, so a typical simulation uses both, e.g. DOFevolvers.overdamped_xvf and DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc.","title":"DOF evolvers"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#The-box-and-boundary-conditions","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"The box is set by sizes = (Lx, Ly, Lz), must all be Floats (!). The system spans  -L/2 to L/2 in each direction. Every particle must start inside it.\n\nPeriodic (Periodic = true): all three directions are periodic, and pair forces use the nearest periodic image of each neighbour. Keep the box length in every direction the particles move in at least twice rcut_pair_global.\nClosed (Periodic = false): If a particle ends up outside the box, the simulation stops with an error.\n\n2D simulations use the same 3D code: put all particles at z = 0, give the box a small Lz=1. (it only needs to be positive), and use forces and evolvers that keep particles in the plane, such as DOFevolvers.overdamped_pqT_xyc for the orientation.","title":"The box and boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Pair-cutoff-and-neighbour-search","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"Pair forces are only evaluated for pairs closer than rcut_pair_global. To find those pairs quickly, JAMS divides the box into cells at least rcut_pair_global wide, and only compares each particle with particles in its own and the neighbouring cells. The cost per timestep then grows linearly with the number of particles.\n\nChoose rcut_pair_global as the range of your longest pair force, and no larger:\n\nfor Forces.repulsive_soft_disk: twice the largest radius\nfor Forces.morse: the distance where the attraction can be neglected\n\nA cutoff that is too small silently cuts off interactions; one that is too large gives the same result but costs more time.","title":"Pair cutoff and neighbour search"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#System:-how-everything-comes-together.","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"The System struct defines the complete system to be simulated, by collecting the system sizes, initial state, the forces, DOF evolvers and cutoff range.","title":"System: how everything comes together."},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Euler_integrator:-evolving-the-system-over-time","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"Euler_integrator loops over t = 0, dt, 2dt, …, t_stop and evolves the System struct over time. It specifies properties of the simulation during runtime, e.g. the plotting and saving. It returns the final state to easily allow chaining simulations.","title":"Euler_integrator: evolving the system over time"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Chaining-simulations","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"Euler_integrator returns a JAMS.SIM with the final state. You can use it as the initial state of a next simulation, for example to relax a packing before switching on activity:\n\nrelaxed = Euler_integrator(passive_system, 0.05, 1000.0)\n\nactive_system = System(\n    sizes = relaxed.system.sizes,\n    initial_particle_state = relaxed.final_particle_state,\n    forces = active_forces,\n    dofevolvers = dofevolvers,\n    Periodic = true,\n    rcut_pair_global = relaxed.system.rcut_pair_global,\n)\nactive = Euler_integrator(active_system, 0.01, 1000.0)\n\nEach simulation starts again at t = 0, so f and T are reset and the unwrapped positions xuw restart from the current x.","title":"Chaining simulations"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Units","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"JAMS has no built-in units: all quantities are numbers in whatever units you choose.","title":"Units"},{"category":"section","location":"overview/#Reproducibility-and-threads","page":"Overview of JAMS","text":"JAMS runs the force, DOF evolver and boundary-condition loops in parallel, on the number of threads Julia was started with e.g. (julia -t 4).\n\nEvery particle gets its own random number generator, seeded from the master seed and the particle id. The random numbers a particle draws therefore do not depend on which thread handles it, and a simulation with the same seed gives the same result on any number of threads.\n\nPass seed to Euler_integrator for a reproducible run. Without it, JAMS picks a random seed and stores it in the output files (integration_info/master_seed), so a saved run can still be repeated.\nEuler_integrator reseeds Julia's global random number generator with this seed. Create your initial conditions before calling it, or seed them separately with Random.seed!.\nIn your own forces, draw random numbers only from rngs_particles[p_i.id], never with a plain rand(), to keep this guarantee.","title":"Reproducibility and threads"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Extending-JAMS","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"JAMS is meant to be easily extended. A new force or DOF evolver consists of the combination of a struct(containing the parameters) plus one method (actually calculating the force). Thanks to Julia's multiple dispatch, the engine picks up the method.","title":"Extending JAMS"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#How-forces-see-a-particle:-p_i","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"Forces and DOF evolvers receive a particle as p_i, a mutable view of particle i of the particle state.\n\nVectors are immutable SVectors, so you always assign a new value (p_i.f += ...) instead of changing an element in place (e.g. p_i.f[1] += ... does not work).","title":"How forces see a particle: p_i"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-external-force","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"An external force acts on one particle at a time. Define a subtype of Forces.ExternalForce and add a method to Forces.contribute_external_force!.\n\nAs an example, a constant force such as gravity needs to add a struct and a method to contributing external forces:\n\nstruct constant_force <: Forces.ExternalForce\n    ontypes::Int\n    F::SVector{3, Float64} #The constant force vector we are adding\nend\n\nfunction contribute_external_force!(p_i, t, dt, rngs_particles, system, force::constant_force)\n    if p_i.type in force.ontypes\n        p_i.f += force.F\n    end\n    return p_i\nend","title":"A custom external force"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-pair-force","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"A pair force acts between two particles closer than rcut_pair_global. Define a subtype of Forces.PairForce and add a method to Forces.contribute_pair_force!. The engine passes both particles, the separation vector dx = x_j - x_i (the nearest periodic image in a periodic box) and its length dxn.\n\nThe engine calls this method once for (i, j) and once for (j, i), possibly on different threads at the same time. So:\n\nonly change p_i, never p_j\ncompute the force on i: dx points from i to j, so a repulsive force has a minus sign","title":"A custom pair force"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-DOF-evolver","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"A local DOF evolver updates one particle from its accumulated force and torque. Define a subtype of DOFevolvers.LocalDOFevolver and add a method to DOFevolvers.evolve_locally!. It must reset the forces or torques it used, otherwise they keep adding up over the timesteps.","title":"A custom DOF evolver"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-particle-type","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"If you need extra degrees of freedom or parameters, define your own particle type. It must have at least these fields, which the engine uses:\n\nField Type Used for\nid Int64 random number generator of the particle; ids must run from 1 to N\ntype Int64 Applying forces and DOFevolvers only to certain types via ontypes of forces and DOF evolvers\nx SVector{3,Float64} position, boundary conditions and neighbour search\nxuw SVector{3,Float64} unwrapped position, set to x at t = 0\nf SVector{3,Float64} total force, reset at t = 0\nT SVector{3,Float64} total torque, reset at t = 0","title":"A custom particle type"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-save-function","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"A save function is called every Tsave timesteps with the HDF5 group of the current frame. Write datasets to it by assigning arrays:\n\nfunction save_positions!(current_frame_group, current_particle_state, current_field_state, n, Tsave, t, framecounter)\n    current_frame_group[\"t\"] = t\n    current_frame_group[\"x\"] = [p_i.x[1] for p_i in current_particle_state]\n    return current_frame_group\nend\n\nPass it as save_functions = (save_positions!,). See Save.polar_particle! for the built-in one.","title":"A custom save function"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#A-custom-plot-function","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"A plot function is called once, when the live plot is set up. It receives the Makie figure and axis, and Observables of the particle and field state. Use @lift to derive the data to plot, so that the plot updates whenever the state changes:\n\n\nfunction points!(f, ax, cpsO, cfsO)\n    x = @lift([p.x[1] for p in $cpsO])\n    y = @lift([p.x[2] for p in $cpsO])\n    scatter!(ax, x, y)\n    return ax\nend","title":"A custom plot function"},{"category":"section","location":"extending/#Tips-for-fast-custom-code","page":"Extending JAMS","text":"The force and evolver methods run for every particle (and every neighbour) in every timestep, so small inefficiencies add up.\n\nUse concrete field types in your structs, or type parameters: struct my_force{T} <: Forces.PairForce; k::T; end. Avoid Any, Real or untyped fields.\nUse SVector for vectors, not Vector. SVector arithmetic does not allocate memory; Vector arithmetic allocates on every call.\nPut forces and evolvers in tuples, not vectors, as in all examples. The engine then compiles one specialized loop for your exact combination.\nDo not use global variables inside forces; store parameters in the struct instead.\nKeep rcut_pair_global as small as your pair forces allow (see Pair cutoff and neighbour search).","title":"Tips for fast custom code"}]
}
